{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION + RESOLUTION: option 1 (bump click in the lock) IS possible and is now implemented. An earlier comment on this ticket concluded option 1 was impossible; that conclusion was based on `constraint-dependencies = [\"click>=8.3.3\"]` returning UNSATISFIABLE. That experiment cannot decide the question: uv constraint-dependencies only NARROWS an existing constraint set, so it can never lift inspect-ai s cap and UNSATISFIABLE was the expected outcome. The correct tool is `override-dependencies`, which REPLACES the offending requirement. Result: `uv lock` -> \"Resolved 167 packages. Updated click v8.2.1 -> v8.4.2\".\n\nROOT CAUSE (agreed, independently proven twice): inspect-ai (via the [eval] extra) requires `click!=8.2.0,<8.2.2,>=8.1.3`, and uv resolves all extras jointly, so 8.2.1 is genuinely the newest click satisfying the set. Proof: deleting the inspect-ai line from pyproject and relocking moves click to 8.4.2 immediately. The uv-version / resolver-mismatch hypothesis is NOT the cause; `uv lock --upgrade-package click` was a no-op because it only permits movement inside the existing constraints.\n\nNO inspect-ai release relaxes the cap - it is identical across every version through the latest 0.3.251. Upstream PR UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_ai#4596 (issue #4594) is open to relax it, unmerged. So waiting for upstream is not an option.\n\nTHE CAP IS STALE, NOT A REAL INCOMPATIBILITY - tested directly: inspect-ai 0.3.251 installed with click 8.4.2 imports cleanly, and `inspect --help` and `inspect eval --help` both render and exit 0. This is what makes the override safe rather than reckless.\n\nFIX: [tool.uv] override-dependencies = [\"click>=8.3.3\"] in pyproject.toml + relocked uv.lock. The override governs this repo s lock/envs only and is NOT published in nava-rebar wheel metadata, so no consumer resolve is affected. pyproject carries the full rationale and a REMOVE-WHEN (drop it once inspect-ai ships a release admitting click >= 8.3.3).\n\nVERIFICATION: `uv lock --check` passes under BOTH uv 0.7.18 and uv 0.12.1 (lock stays at revision 2, so CI-safe whatever setup-uv v9 installs); `uv sync --locked --extra dev` resolves and installs click 8.4.2; `pip-audit --desc` over that whole environment reports \"No known vulnerabilities found\", so the old pin was masking nothing else; 1463 selected tests pass, 0 failures.\n\nDIFF SCOPE: click 8.2.1->8.4.2, the lock manifest override line, and one stale zipfile-zstd marker that any relock corrects (uv 0.7.18 and 0.12.1 agree on it). The other 166 packages are untouched.\n\nOption 2 (--ignore-vuln PYSEC-2026-2132) is therefore NOT needed and is deliberately declined: it would suppress a real advisory and weaken a gating security check when a clean fix exists."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785712828605720001,"uuid":"7f4d824b-957a-4803-bb97-299dc94d6878"}